# Host

Every call that reaches outside the program lives in this file: files, the
environment, other programs and the network. Everything else in the factory is
written against these functions and nothing below them, so the rest of the code
reads as policy and is tested as policy, and the edge where the world comes in is
one page long.

The file calls work in a test's sealed world (an in-memory filesystem), so the
modules that keep state on disk are tested without touching a disk. A sealed world
starts no process and opens no connection: the functions that run `largo` or talk
to OpenRouter are exercised by the live tests (`largo test --live`), which say so.

Import Shared.

## Note
Where the factory finds its tools and how far it trusts them to run: the `largo`
it checks proofs with (`MACSAD_LARGO`, else `largo` on the `PATH`), the deadline
of one kernel run, the output it keeps from one, and the directory it writes the
documents it checks into (`MACSAD_WORK`, else `.macsad-work`).

## A Toolchain has:
    a largo, which is Text.
    a kernelTimeoutMs, which is Int.
    a maxOutput, which is Int.
    a workDir, which is Text.

## Note
The value of an environment variable, or `fallback` when it is unset or empty.

## To envOr (name: Text, fallback: Text) -> Text:
    Inspect get(name):
        When Some (v):
            If trim(v) is equal to "":
                Return fallback.
            Return v.
        When None:
            Return fallback.
    Return fallback.

## Note
The toolchain the environment describes.

## To defaultToolchain () -> Toolchain:
    Return a new Toolchain with largo envOr("MACSAD_LARGO", "largo") and kernelTimeoutMs 120000 and maxOutput 4194304 and workDir envOr("MACSAD_WORK", ".macsad-work").

## Note
A file's text.

## To readText (path: Text) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Return read(path).

## Note
A file's text replaced.

## To writeText (path: Text, content: Text) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Return write(path, content).

## Note
Text added to the end of a file (created when absent): how every ledger in the
factory grows, so an entry once written is never rewritten.

## To appendText (path: Text, content: Text) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Return appendFile(path, content).

## Note
Whether anything is at a path.

## To pathExists (path: Text) -> Bool:
    Return fileExists(path).

## Note
The sorted names in a directory.

## To listNames (dir: Text) -> Result of Seq of Text and Text:
    Return listDir(dir).

## Note
A directory and every directory above it.

## To ensureDir (dir: Text) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Return makeDirs(dir).

## Note
A file replaced all at once: written beside itself under a temporary name, then
renamed over the old one, so a reader sees the old content or the new and never
half of either.

## To writeAtomically (path: Text, content: Text) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Let temporary be path + ".tmp".
    Inspect write(temporary, content):
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.
        When Ok (done):
            Return renameFile(temporary, path).
    Return a new Err with error "unwritten".

## Note
The program's arguments, the program's own name first.

## To programArgs () -> Seq of Text:
    Return args().

## Note
The time, in Unix milliseconds.

## To clockMs () -> Int:
    Return now().

## Note
One run of `largo` with `args`, bounded by the toolchain's deadline and output cap.

## To runLargo (tc: Toolchain, arguments: Seq of Text) -> Result of ProcessResult and Text:
    Return runProcess(tc's largo, arguments, "", a new Map of Text to Text, tc's kernelTimeoutMs, tc's maxOutput).

## Note
A program run with `arguments`, for the tools the factory drives besides `largo`
(`gzip`, `split`, `git`).

## To runTool (program: Text, arguments: Seq of Text, timeoutMs: Int, maxOutput: Int) -> Result of ProcessResult and Text:
    Return runProcess(program, arguments, "", a new Map of Text to Text, timeoutMs, maxOutput).

## Note
What one kernel run answered: its exit status, its report, and whether its
deadline stopped it — the three things `kernelVerdict` reads.

## A KernelRun has:
    an exitCode, which is Int.
    a stdout, which is Text.
    a timedOut, which is Bool.

## Note
`document` checked by the kernel: written into the work directory under its own
hash (so a document is checked from the bytes it hashes to, and two identical
documents share one file), then `largo prove --kernel <file> --json`.

## To kernelRun (tc: Toolchain, document: Text) -> Result of KernelRun and Text:
    Inspect makeDirs(tc's workDir):
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "cannot make the work directory: " + e.
        When Ok (made):
            Let path be tc's workDir + "/" + sha256Hex(document) + ".lg".
            Inspect write(path, document):
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error "cannot write the document: " + e.
                When Ok (written):
                    Inspect runLargo(tc, ["prove", "--kernel", path, "--json"]):
                        When Err (e):
                            Return a new Err with error "cannot run largo: " + e.
                        When Ok (r):
                            Return a new Ok with value (a new KernelRun with exitCode r's exitCode and stdout r's stdout and timedOut r's timedOut).
    Return a new Err with error "unrun".

## Note
The key a worker calls OpenRouter with. It is read here, at the moment of the
call, and handed to one header; nothing stores it.

## To openRouterKey () -> Option of Text:
    Return get("OPENROUTER_API_KEY").

## Note
One HTTP exchange.

## To httpCall (method: Text, url: Text, headers: Map of Text to Text, body: Text, timeoutMs: Int) -> Result of HttpReply and Text:
    Return httpRequest(method, url, headers, body, timeoutMs).

## Test: the toolchain falls back to largo on the PATH and a local work directory

Let tc be defaultToolchain().
Expect that tc's largo is equal to "largo".
Expect that tc's workDir is equal to ".macsad-work".
Expect that tc's kernelTimeoutMs is equal to 120000.

## Test: an atomic write replaces a file whole and leaves no temporary behind

Inspect writeAtomically("ledger.json", "first"):
    When Ok (a):
        Let ignored be a.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "written".
Inspect writeAtomically("ledger.json", "second"):
    When Ok (b):
        Let ignored be b.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "written".
Inspect readText("ledger.json"):
    When Ok (t):
        Expect that t is equal to "second".
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "readable".
Expect that pathExists("ledger.json.tmp") is equal to false.

## Test: appending grows a file one entry at a time

Inspect ensureDir("trails/opdp"):
    When Ok (d):
        Let ignored be d.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "made".
Inspect appendText("trails/opdp/116.jsonl", "one\n"):
    When Ok (a):
        Let ignored be a.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "appended".
Inspect appendText("trails/opdp/116.jsonl", "two\n"):
    When Ok (a):
        Let ignored be a.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "appended".
Inspect readText("trails/opdp/116.jsonl"):
    When Ok (t):
        Expect that t is equal to "one\ntwo\n".
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "readable".
Inspect listNames("trails/opdp"):
    When Ok (names):
        Expect that names is equal to ["116.jsonl"].
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "listed".

## Test: a sealed world starts no process

Inspect runTool("true", [], 1000, 1000):
    When Ok (r):
        Expect that r's exitCode is equal to -1.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that length of e is greater than 0.

## Test: the kernel checks a recorded fixture document for real

This test may use the real filesystem.
This test may use the real processes.
This test may use the real environment.
Let tc be defaultToolchain().
Inspect readText("tests/fixtures/kernel/verified.lg"):
    When Ok (doc):
        Inspect kernelRun(tc, doc):
            When Ok (run):
                Expect that run's exitCode is equal to 0.
                Expect that run's timedOut is equal to false.
                Expect that run's stdout contains "'proof_of_goal' depends on no axioms".
            When Err (e):
                Expect that e is equal to "a run of " + tc's largo.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "readable".
